The GitHub Model Context Protocol (MCP) server supports two primary authentication methods: OAuth and Personal Access Tokens (PATs) [1][2].

For the remote GitHub MCP server (hosted at https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/), OAuth is the default and recommended method, allowing for a one-click sign-in experience within supported IDEs [1][3]. If you prefer using a PAT, you can manually configure it by adding an Authorization header to your MCP server configuration [1][2].

```json
{
  "servers": {
    "github": {
      "url": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/",
      "requestInit": {
        "headers": {
          "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR_GITHUB_PAT"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

For local (stdio) MCP server deployments, OAuth is supported via a browser-based flow that keeps tokens in memory [4][2]. You can also use a PAT by setting the `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable, which takes precedence over OAuth [4][2]. When using your own OAuth or GitHub App for custom deployments, you must provide the client ID and secret via command-line flags or environment variables [4].

The Klaviyo Model Context Protocol (MCP) server allows AI clients to interact with your Klaviyo data by acting as a wrapper for Klaviyo APIs [1][2]. To authenticate the MCP server, you can use either OAuth or a private API key depending on your setup [1][3].

For most MCP clients, you configure the server by providing a private API key in the environment variables of your configuration file [1][3]. Use the following structure for your configuration:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "klaviyo": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["klaviyo-mcp-server@latest"],
      "env": {
        "PRIVATE_API_KEY": "YOUR_API_KEY",
        "READ_ONLY": "false",
        "ALLOW_USER_GENERATED_CONTENT": "false"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Alternatively, when using custom connectors in platforms like Claude or ChatGPT, you can select OAuth authentication and follow the prompted authorization flow to grant the AI access to your account [1][3]. Ensure you have an Owner, Admin, or Manager role to manage these credentials [1][4][2].
